From: Protector of Earth Recomp
Subject: [PATCH] VU1 interpreter: upper and lower executors as templates on the instruction word

What it is for:
  Translated VU1 code builds one function per distinct upper and lower
  word by inlining the executor with a constant word, and relied on the
  compiler dropping every opcode case the word cannot reach. GCC and Clang
  do that before inlining; MSVC inlines first: with the FMAC model forced
  inline (patch 0020) one generated file needed more than 30 GB, and
  without it (patch 0023) the model ran out of line, about a quarter of
  the Ben 10 port's game thread on Windows.

The change:
  - execUpperT<kConst, kInstr> and execLowerT<kConst, kInstr>: the
    executors' bodies, with every opcode case group (main opcode, lower
    special funct and funct2, upper special op) under
    `if constexpr (PS2_VU_UPPER_CASE(...))` and the like. With kConst
    false every condition is true: execUpper and execLowerInline call
    <false, 0> and behave as before. With kConst true only the cases of
    kInstr are instantiated, and the fields and dest mask are constants.
    Case groups without statements still fall through; every other group
    already ended in return or break.
  - VU1NativeAccess::execUpperWord<W> and VU1NativeLowerAccess::
    execLowerWord<W> for translated code.
  - PS2_VU_FMAC_INLINE is the forced inline on every compiler again
    (patch 0023 made it plain inline on MSVC).
  Same results. Tested: ps2xTest 454/454; the Ben 10 port's 40 captured
  VU1 workloads give the same checksums and cycle count with and without
  its translated code; GCC 13: one generated file compiles with 529 MB at
  most (711 MB before).

--- a/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ps2_vu1.h
+++ b/ps2xRuntime/include/runtime/ps2_vu1.h
@@ -20,17 +20,12 @@
 #define PS2_VU_FORCEINLINE inline __attribute__((always_inline))
 #endif
 
-// The FMAC result and flag model (runtime/ps2_vu1_fmac_inl.h): forced inline
-// on GCC and Clang, which drop the unused opcode cases of a constant upper
-// word before inlining. MSVC inlines first and prunes afterwards: forcing
-// the model into every case of the upper executor made one file of
-// translated code need more than 30 GB to compile, so there the compiler
-// decides.
-#if defined(_MSC_VER)
-#define PS2_VU_FMAC_INLINE inline
-#else
+// The FMAC result and flag model (runtime/ps2_vu1_fmac_inl.h), forced inline.
+// Translated code compiles one opcode case per upper word (execUpperT), so
+// each copy of the model is for one instruction. (Before that, MSVC inlined
+// the model into all ~70 cases of every translated word before dropping the
+// unused ones, and one file needed more than 30 GB: findings/21 s.6.)
 #define PS2_VU_FMAC_INLINE PS2_VU_FORCEINLINE
-#endif
 
